1. RAG Fundamentals (rag_fundamentals)Basic Q1: Explain the core difference between Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) and fine-tuning when it comes to updating factual knowledge. Why is RAG generally preferred for frequently changing data?Grounded Answer/Grading Guide: Fine-tuning modifies model behavior or style, but it is expensive to update and is brittle for fact memorisation1. Its knowledge remains frozen at a specific training cutoff date2more_horiz. RAG decouple facts from the model weights by fetching real-time data dynamically from an un-frozen external database and inserting it into the prompt1more_horiz. Upgrading or updating facts in RAG is cheap and fast—you simply re-embed the updated documents1.Basic Q2: Describe the three core steps—Retrieve, Augment, and Generate—in a classic RAG loop. What is the operational purpose of each?Grounded Answer/Grading Guide:Retrieve: Search and extract the top-k text chunks from an external vector index or keyword store that look most relevant to the query7.Augment: Assemble the retrieved text chunks alongside the original user query and system instructions into a single structured prompt7.Generate: Pass the augmented prompt to the LLM, which acts as a reasoning engine to write a response grounded strictly in the provided text68.Intermediate Q1: A project manager suggests getting rid of your RAG retrieval pipeline and instead stuffing all 200,000 words of internal documents directly into a new, long-context LLM prompt. Explain three distinct practical reasons why this brute-force approach fails in production.Grounded Answer/Grading Guide:Cost: Processing massive context lengths on every user query is astronomically expensive at scale59.Latency: Extremely large prompt inputs make model calls slow, degrading the user experience9.Quality/Lost in the Middle: LLMs actually perform worse when overloaded with noisy, irrelevant context10. They exhibit a "lost in the middle" U-curve, where they fail to reason or find details buried in the middle of long prompts11more_horiz.Intermediate Q2: Explain how the probabilistic nature of Large Language Models contributes to hallucinations in the absence of a retrieval layer.Grounded Answer/Grading Guide: LLMs do not verify factual databases; they assign probabilities to possible next-token continuations based on their training corpus (which can contain errors, biases, or contradictions)1415. Due to sampling randomness (such as temperature or top-k settings) or vague prompts, models may choose incorrect or fabricated continuations14. Because they cannot differentiate between what they "know" and what they do not, they generate these incorrect completions with high linguistic confidence, resulting in hallucinations1516.Advanced Q1: Compare RAG with Fine-Tuning across the following five dimensions: fact updates, behavior/style control, cost of iteration, source traceability, and data privacy. Describe a production scenario where combining both techniques is necessary.Grounded Answer/Grading Guide: RAG excels at factual updates, is cheap to update, preserves source traceability (using chunk IDs), and keeps private data secure within your storage boundary, but has limited behavioral/style control and introduces retrieval latency1. Fine-tuning is great for altering behavior/tone, has no retrieval latency, but is expensive to train, poor at updating facts, and lacks source traceability1. Combined Scenario: You would combine both when building a highly specialized agent (e.g., a corporate legal advisor) that must strictly follow custom formatting, legal vocabulary, and specific tones (Fine-Tuning) but must ground its advice in constantly updating, private contract documents (RAG)117.2. Data Ingestion & Chunking (chunking)Basic Q1: Explain the "precision-context trade-off" when selecting an ingestion chunk size. What is the performance risk of chunks that are too small versus chunks that are too large?Grounded Answer/Grading Guide: Chunks that are too large muddle vector search embeddings by mixing multiple topics together, resulting in "averaged" noisy vectors that are hard to match1819. They also waste context space1920. Chunks that are too small create precise vectors that match easily but lack the surrounding context the LLM needs to generate a logically complete and meaningful response11more_horiz.Basic Q2: What is "chunk overlap," why is it necessary for simple fixed-size token chunking, and what are typical default parameters used for a baseline production system?Grounded Answer/Grading Guide: Chunk overlap is the repeating of a percentage of tokens from the end of one chunk at the beginning of the next10more_horiz. It serves as a safety net to prevent cutting mid-sentence, mid-word, or mid-table, bridging facts that would otherwise be split and lost across boundaries10more_horiz. A typical production baseline is a chunk size of 512 tokens with an overlap of 50–100 tokens (approx. 10%–20%)22more_horiz.Intermediate Q1: Contrast the architectural approach and trade-offs of "Pre-chunking" versus "Post-chunking" (chunk-on-demand) in ingestion pipelines.Grounded Answer/Grading Guide: Pre-chunking processes documents asynchronously offline, breaking them down and embedding them before storing them in a vector database26. It enables very fast query search because chunks are pre-computed, but requires rigid, upfront decisions on chunk sizes26. Post-chunking embeds entire documents first and dynamically chunks them at query-time only on the retrieved set27. It allows for flexible, query-aware chunking strategies but introduces latency on initial access and requires complex caching27.Intermediate Q2: Walk through the operational mechanics of Semantic Chunking. How does it dynamically determine where to split text without relying on fixed token or character limits?Grounded Answer/Grading Guide: Semantic chunking groups text based on meaning rather than rules28. It works by:Segmenting the text into individual sentences28.Generating vector embeddings for each sentence28.Walking the document and calculating the cosine similarity between adjacent sentences2829.Placing Topic Breakpoints (cuts) at intervals where similarity drops sharply2829.Grouping consecutive same-topic sentences together into logically coherent chunks2829.Advanced Q1: Contrast Hierarchical (Parent-Child) chunking with Late Chunking. How does each strategy structurally resolve the precision-context trade-off?Grounded Answer/Grading Guide: Hierarchical chunking splits text into multiple layers30. It embeds and searches highly granular "child" chunks (~150 tokens) to achieve high-precision vector matches, but retrieves and passes the larger "parent" chunk (~1000 tokens) to the LLM to supply full generating context2131. Late Chunking runs the entire document through a long-context embedding model first, generating token-level embeddings that possess global awareness of the entire document3233. Chunks are then split, and their embeddings are derived by averaging those contextualized token vectors33. This ensures individual chunks retain global context without needing to retrieve larger text spans3233.3. Vector Embeddings & Latent Space (embeddings)Basic Q1: What is a "latent space" (or embedding space) mathematically, and how does a trained encoder model represent semantic similarity geometrically within it?Grounded Answer/Grading Guide: A latent space is a high-dimensional manifold where words, sentences, or documents are projected as vectors (a long sequence of numbers)3435. Geometric proximity in this space directly reflects semantic similarity; sentences that have similar meanings are positioned closer to one another than semantically different concepts3536.Basic Q2: Compare Euclidean distance and Cosine similarity as metrics to evaluate vector proximity. Under what scenario is Cosine similarity preferred?Grounded Answer/Grading Guide: Euclidean distance measures the straight, shortest geometric path between two vector coordinates37. Cosine similarity measures the angle alignment between vectors (by projecting them onto a unit circle and measuring distance along the arc), normalizing vector magnitude37. Cosine similarity is preferred when the engineer is concerned with relative semantic weighting and topical alignment rather than the raw magnitude or document length37.Intermediate Q1: Explain the sequential evolution of text representation models from RNNs (Fully-Recurrent Neural Networks) to LSTMs, and finally to Transformers. What parallelization bottleneck did Transformers solve?Grounded Answer/Grading Guide: RNNs process tokens sequentially, where each token feeds forward into the next, but they suffer from vanishing gradients which prevents them from capturing long-distance relationships in text38more_horiz. LSTMs mitigate vanishing gradients using parallel memory cells and gating systems, but they must calculate token-by-token sequentially, creating a massive serial training bottleneck4041. Transformers replace this sequential structure with attention mechanisms (Query, Key, Value vectors), allowing the model to weigh all tokens simultaneously and compute representations in parallel across GPUs4142.Intermediate Q2: State the non-negotiable rule of embedding models in a production system. What happens if this rule is broken, what is the symptom, and how do you resolve it?Grounded Answer/Grading Guide: The "golden rule" is that you must strictly lock your embedding model version43. Query and chunk vectors must come from the exact same model43. If this is violated (e.g., query embedded with Model B but index built with Model A), the vectors exist in different dimensional vector spaces, leading to completely garbage search results43. Resolving a violation requires re-embedding the entire document corpus from scratch using the new model43.Advanced Q1: Detail how a Transformer-based encoder model utilizes Query, Key, and Value vectors to update a token's representation. How is a single dense vector representing an entire sentence or sequence ultimately extracted for RAG?Grounded Answer/Grading Guide: The attention mechanism learns Query ($Q$), Key ($K$), and Value ($V$) vectors for every token in a sequence42. The similarity between one token's $Q$ vector and another's $K$ vector mathematically determines how much of the second token's $V$ vector gets added to the first, dynamically updating its semantic meaning based on its sequential context4244. To extract a single sequence-level vector, the model takes the final representation of a designated token: masked models (like BERT) extract a special [CLS] token, while autoregressive models (like GPT) extract the very last token in the sequence45.4. Retrieval & Search Architectures (retrieval_search)Basic Q1: Compare dense retrieval (semantic vector search) with sparse retrieval (lexical keyword search like BM25). For which query types does each excel in production?Grounded Answer/Grading Guide: Dense retrieval excels at synonym matching, paraphrasing, multilingual mapping, and handling typos46more_horiz. Sparse retrieval (BM25) excels at matching exact alphanumeric strings, part numbers, product SKUs, error codes, legal citation numbers, acronyms, and rare proper nouns that the embedding model has never seen47more_horiz.Basic Q2: What is Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF), and how does a hybrid retriever use it to merge dense and sparse search outputs?Grounded Answer/Grading Guide: RRF is a rank-based fusion algorithm that calculates document relevance scores based strictly on their positions in the dense and sparse search lists rather than their raw scores: $\text{Score}(d) = \sum \frac{1}{k + \text{rank}_i(d)}$, where a constant $k \approx 60$ is typical5152. It is a robust default because it bypasses the issue of merging completely different score scales (cosine vs. lexical scores) and requires no complex tuning52.Intermediate Q1: Describe the two-pass retrieval architecture of Bi-encoders versus Cross-encoders. Why is the Bi-encoder used for the first pass (retrieval) and the Cross-encoder reserved for the second pass (reranking)?Grounded Answer/Grading Guide: Bi-encoders encode queries and documents separately, allowing document vectors to be pre-computed at ingestion; searching is a fast vector similarity calculation suitable for querying millions of documents in milliseconds (high recall, first pass)53. Cross-encoders feed the query and chunk together into a single Transformer, allowing full joint attention across both sequences53. This provides incredibly high precision but is computationally slow, making it feasible only as a second-pass ranker on a small candidate subset (e.g., top 50 chunks)5354.Intermediate Q2: Explain the concept of Hypothetical Document Embeddings (HyDE). What latent space problem does it solve, and how does it work?Grounded Answer/Grading Guide: Problem: User queries and source documents look geometrically different in latent space (a question vector does not naturally align with a narrative answer vector)5255. Solution: HyDE uses the LLM to write a hypothetical "ideal answer" to the query before search occurs5255. This fake answer is then embedded and used as the search vector5255. Because a fake answer structurally matches the document corpus better than a question, retrieval recall improves significantly5255.Advanced Q1: You are designing a vector index that must scale to hundreds of millions of chunks. Compare the indexing strategies of HNSW (Hierarchical Navigable Small World), IVF (Inverted File), and PQ (Product Quantization) in terms of memory overhead, search speed, and recall accuracy.Grounded Answer/Grading Guide:HNSW: Builds a multi-layer graph index. It provides exceptionally fast search speeds and the highest recall accuracy, but has massive RAM memory overhead5657.IVF (Inverted File): Clusters the vector space to search only the closest cluster centroids57. It reduces search calculations and RAM overhead compared to HNSW, but degrades recall accuracy slightly57.PQ (Product Quantization): Compresses vectors into small byte-sized representations57. It reduces index storage sizes by 10-30x (extremely memory efficient) but introduces quantisation noise that lowers search precision57.5. Context Window Engineering (context_engineering)Basic Q1: How does the discipline of Context Engineering differ from Prompt Engineering? Provide a concrete example of an engineering decision that falls under context engineering.Grounded Answer/Grading Guide: Prompt engineering focuses on phrasing, tone, formatting, and single-turn instructions58more_horiz. Context engineering is the system architecture of selecting, filtering, compressing, and structuring what enters the finite context window to optimize the LLM's attention budget60more_horiz. Examples: implementing hierarchical child-parent retrievers64, defining vector memory tiers6365, or setting up KV-cache prompt stack layouts6667.Basic Q2: Describe the "Lost in the Middle" attention curve shown by long-context models. How should an engineer order retrieved context chunks in a prompt stack to fight this?Grounded Answer/Grading Guide: Models are highly accurate at processing information near the very beginning (primacy) or very end (recency) of a long context window, but their reasoning and retrieval accuracy drop sharply for information positioned in the middle11more_horiz. To mitigate this, place your most critical, high-scoring retrieved chunks at the very top and very bottom of the context block, placing weaker candidates in the middle13more_horiz.Intermediate Q1: What is "Context Rot" in long-turn agent interactions? List three symptoms and three architectural mitigations.Grounded Answer/Grading Guide: Context rot occurs as a context window fills up with long-running sequences of text69.Symptoms: Generative repetition, instruction drift/forgetting, and loss of precision69.Mitigations: Summarising or compressing stale conversational history69, applying rerankers to retrieve fewer but higher-quality chunks69, repeating key system instructions near the end of the prompt69, or storing active states in external databases rather than stuffing them in-context69.Intermediate Q2: Walk through the structural layout of a production-grade Prompt Stack from top to bottom. Why are "stable" prefixes placed on top and "volatile" queries placed at the bottom?Grounded Answer/Grading Guide: Layout from top to bottom:System Instructions (stable)70.Few-Shot Examples (stable)67.Compressed conversational history67.XML-delimited retrieved context chunks1367.Tool output observations67.Refusal rule reminders67.User query (volatile)67. Caching Principle: Placing static, unchanged instructions (system prompts, few-shots) at the top allows hosting frameworks to reuse the Key-Value (KV) cache across calls, dramatically lowering processing costs and query latency6667.Advanced Q1: Detail the operational mechanics and trade-offs of the following three context compression techniques: Contextual Extraction, Query-Aware Summarization, and Token-Level Prompt Compression (e.g., LLMLingua).Grounded Answer/Grading Guide:Contextual Extraction: Uses a lightweight model to extract only the specific sentences within a retrieved chunk that answer the user query71. It preserves exact original wording (retaining citability) and achieves 5-10x token reduction, but adds another model call to latency71.Query-Aware Summarization: Condenses long paragraphs or meeting transcripts into summaries tailored strictly to the query71. It offers massive token savings but carries the risk of summary hallucination71.Token-Level Prompt Compression: Uses mathematical heuristics to delete low-information tokens (fillers) at the model's vocabulary level without semantic rewriting70. It achieves 2-10x compression and is fast, but it can break structured formatting and makes prompts harder to debug70.6. RAG Evaluation & Benchmarking (rag_evaluation)Basic Q1: Why is it critical to decouple the evaluation of your Retrieval component from your Generation component? What is the risk of relying only on end-to-end answer correctness scores?Grounded Answer/Grading Guide: An end-to-end score hides where a system broke72. If an answer is wrong, you cannot isolate whether it was a Retrieval Failure (the correct chunk was never retrieved) or a Generation Failure (the correct chunk was retrieved but the LLM ignored/misused it)7273. Decoupling allows you to diagnose and fix the correct subsystem (e.g., tuning chunking/rerankers for retrieval vs. modifying system instructions for generation)73.Basic Q2: Define and explain the specific evaluation roles of the following retrieval metrics: Recall@k, Mean Reciprocal Rank (MRR), and Mean Average Precision (MAP).Grounded Answer/Grading Guide:Recall@k: Measures the proportion of times the correct target document is retrieved somewhere within the top-k results73more_horiz.MRR: Evaluates search position quality by calculating the reciprocal rank of the first relevant document retrieved, penalizing systems that push the true answer down the list7475.MAP: Evaluates systems where multiple relevant chunks exist, averaging precision across all relevant ranks to measure search completeness7475.Intermediate Q1: Detail the step-by-step methodology for synthetically generating a grounded "golden dataset" for RAG evaluation using an LLM.Grounded Answer/Grading Guide:Extract clean, structured text from your production knowledge documents7677.Pass these document contexts to a high-performing LLM and instruct it to generate targeted question-and-answer pairs that can only be answered by the provided text76more_horiz.Pass the generated question-context pairs to independent LLM "critique agents"7680.Filter and discard any QA pairs that fail critique checks to ensure high-quality test data7781.Intermediate Q2: Explain the concepts and metrics used by the three critique filters: Groundedness, Relevance, and Stand-alone when synthesizing evaluation datasets.Groundedness: Ensures the question can be fully and factually answered using only the provided context document, preventing out-of-bounds questions80.Relevance: Evaluates if the question is useful and realistic for a human user to ask, discarding useless or highly obscure questions80.Stand-alone: Verifies that the question is completely understandable in isolation without needing the original document to provide context (e.g., discarding questions with deictic references like "What function is used in this guide?")81.Advanced Q1: Design an automated LLM-as-a-judge evaluation rubric to measure the "Faithfulness" of generated answers. How must the evaluator's prompt be structured to maximize scoring consistency and prevent arbitrary ratings?Grounded Answer/Grading Guide: The evaluation pipeline must:Provide the judge with a highly descriptive, concrete rubric mapping each point of a 1-5 scale to precise factual conditions (modeled after Prometheus templates), preventing vague interpretations82.Structure the judge's prompt to enforce Chain-of-Thought (CoT), strictly requiring the model to write out its reasoning, evidence, and rationale before it outputs the final numeric score8182. This gives the model more active tokens to "think" and structure its judgment, maximizing scoring consistency8182.7. Production Scaling & Failure Mitigations (production_rag)Basic Q1: Explain how Metadata Filtering operates in a production database, and why it is critical for enforcing multi-tenant ACL (Access Control List) security.Grounded Answer/Grading Guide: Metadata filtering applies structured attributes (like tenant_id or acl_roles) directly to the chunk vector at ingestion time8384. During queries, the index filters out candidates based on these attributes before approximate nearest neighbor (ANN) vector search runs8485. This ensures that unauthorized chunks are structurally invisible to the search engine, preventing data leaks84. The engineer must never rely on prompt-level instructions to enforce security boundaries86.Basic Q2: List three major failure modes of a RAG pipeline in production (excluding security leaks) and provide a targeted architectural mitigation for each.Grounded Answer/Grading Guide:Wrong chunks retrieved (fluent but off-topic answers): Add sparse (BM25) search to build a hybrid retriever, or use query rewriting87.Right chunk retrieved but ranked too low (LLM misses it): Add a second-pass cross-encoder reranker to force relevant context to the top87.Stale index (system references outdated files): Implement webhook event-driven reindexing on document updates, and use version/date metadata filters88.Intermediate Q1: You are given a latency budget of ~800ms for a production RAG application. Draft a realistic back-of-the-napkin budget distribution across the query pipeline. Which component dominates this budget, and how do you optimize it?Grounded Answer/Grading Guide: Budget breakdown:Query embedding: ~80ms89.Vector search: ~60ms89.BM25 search: ~45ms89.Fusion: ~10ms89.Reranking (50 to 5 candidates): ~150ms89.LLM Generation: ~400ms (Time-to-First-Token)89.Overhead/Network: ~60ms89.Optimization: LLM generation dominates the budget (~50%)89. To optimize, use smaller, highly optimized models for routine queries, implement token-level prompt compression, or enforce strict output token limits67more_horiz.Intermediate Q2: Analyze the financial cost budget of a production RAG pipeline. Contrast the cost contributions of embeddings, database retrieval, reranking, and generation. Where does the dominant cost lie?Grounded Answer/Grading Guide:Query embedding: Extremely cheap (prompt-only pricing, ~$0.00002)89.Vector retrieval: Negligible (amortized database infrastructure cost, ~$0.0001)89.Reranking: Low (approx. ~$0.001 per call with APIs or virtually free if self-hosted)89.LLM Generation: Dominates costs entirely (e.g., ~$0.02 per query for a GPT-4-class model processing 2,000 input tokens and 300 output tokens)89.Conclusion: Over 95% of RAG query costs come from generation tokens89. The dominant cost is optimized by choosing smaller, cheaper model classes for routine questions or using prompt compression6987.Advanced Q1: Draw and describe a comprehensive production-grade blueprint that maps out an offline ingest pipeline and an online query pipeline. Detail how asynchronous updates, vector caching, and multi-stage reranking are architected.Grounded Answer/Grading Guide:[OFFLINE INGEST]
Raw Documents -> Layout-Aware Parser/OCR -> Chunking & Metadata (tenant, ACL) -> GPU Batch Embedder -> Vector DB / BM25 Index
                          ^                                                             |
                   Webhook/Cron Updates ------------------------------------------------+

[ONLINE QUERY]
User Query -> LLM Rewriter/Expansion -> Embedder -> Vector & BM25 Search + Metadata Filter -> RRF Fusion (top 50) -> Reranker -> Prompt Stack (KV-cached) -> LLM Generation -> Streaming Output & Observability Logs
Asynchronous Updates: Handled via event-driven webhooks or nightly sync cron jobs that dynamically re-embed changed documents, utilizing soft-deletes and version tags to prevent downtime8890. Security: Metadata filtering (ACL, tenant isolation) is enforced at the database layer before ANN calculation to prevent cross-tenant leakage8486. Query Pipeline: User query is normalized via query rewriting (HyDE)5286, embedded, and retrieved using hybrid dense-sparse search5190. Candidates are fused via RRF, filtered by security metadata, and passed to a cross-encoder reranker to extract the top 5 chunks5186. These are placed in a prompt stack (system instructions on top to maximize KV-cache hits) and sent to the LLM67. The generation is streamed to the user and fully logged for tracing90.
